  "worked_solution": "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(r,\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{r}^{4}} & ={sin}^{2}\u20612\\theta  \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={r}^{4}{sin}^{2}\u20612\\theta  \\\\ z & ={r}^{2}|\\operatorname{sin}\u20612\\theta | \\\\ z & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u20612\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z\\ge 0.$ Also, we drop the absolute value bars because $\\operatorname{sin}\u20612\\theta \\ge 0$ for  $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$  \n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=\u27e8x,\\,y,\\,z\u27e9=\u27e8r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,z\u27e9.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u20612\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=\u27e8r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u20612\\theta \u27e9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is ${r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u20612\\theta .$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
